section: cloud-services
title: Cloud services and APIs
about: |
  The APIs and the services of the cloud that a build calls. Palette does not check that one is
  on before it starts.
rules:
- id: gke-api-off
  title: The Kubernetes Engine API is on in the GCP project
  engines:
  - gke
  tier: cloud
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    With the Kubernetes Engine API off, the cluster waits at `PreparingInfrastructure` for 30
    minutes or more with no error in its status. One event names it: "Kubernetes Engine API has
    not been used in project ... or it is disabled".
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - gcloud
    argv:
    - gcloud
    - services
    - list
    - --enabled
    - --project
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }}'
    - --filter
    - config.name=container.googleapis.com
    - --format
    - json
    expr: |
      if .exec.exit != 0 then ["gcloud services list failed: \(.exec.stderr | .[:200])"]
      elif ((.exec.stdout // []) | length) == 0 then ["container.googleapis.com is not enabled in project \(.entity.spec.cloudConfig.project)"]
      else [] end
  reproduce:
  - gcloud services list --enabled --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} | grep -E 'container|compute'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: The GKE create calls the GCP property route, and the route checks Service Usage.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#90'
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gke/api-off.json
    clean: fixtures/gke/clean.json
- id: gcp-apis-off
  title: The Compute, Cloud Resource Manager, and IAM APIs are on in the GCP project
  engines:
  - gcp
  - gke
  tier: cloud
  severity: warn
  status: source
  what: |
    The documentation asks for the Compute API, the Cloud Resource Manager API, and the IAM API.
    A call to one that is off fails with `SERVICE_DISABLED`. A GKE build needs the Kubernetes
    Engine API (`gke-api-off`) and the Compute API. The GCP property route of Palette calls
    Cloud Resource Manager, and a GCP IaaS create calls that route; a GKE create does not. The
    #90 GKE clusters were built in a project with Cloud Resource Manager off, so a missing API
    here is a warning to read, not a proof. `gcp-properties` asks the property route of Palette for
    the project and the zones, with a key alone.
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - gcloud
    argv:
    - gcloud
    - services
    - list
    - --enabled
    - --project
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }}'
    - --format
    - json
    expr: |
      if .exec.exit != 0 then ["gcloud services list failed: \(.exec.stderr | .[:200])"]
      else ([ (.exec.stdout // [])[] | .config.name ]) as $on
        | [ "compute.googleapis.com", "cloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com", "iam.googleapis.com" ]
        | map(select(. as $s | ($on | index($s)) == null) | "\(.) is not enabled in the project")
      end
  reproduce:
  - gcloud services list --enabled --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --format='value(config.name)' | grep -E 'compute|cloudresourcemanager|iam.googleapis'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Check Service Usage in the GCP property route.
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/clusters/public-cloud/gcp/add-gcp-accounts/
  - kind: run
    ref: preflight-plan gke 20260929T043631Z
    note: cloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com was off in the project that built the
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gke/apis-off.json
    clean: fixtures/gke/clean.json
- id: gcp-properties
  title: The property route accepts the project and the zones of a GCP cluster
  engines:
  - gcp
  tier: palette
  severity: fail
  status: source
  when: .entity.spec.cloudAccountUid != null and (.entity.spec.cloudConfig.project // "") != ""
  what: |
    `POST /v1/clouds/gcp/properties/validate` reads the project with Cloud Resource Manager and
    each zone with Compute, under the cloud account. Palette refuses a project that the account
    cannot read, and a zone that the project does not hold. The console calls the route on a GCP
    create, and not on a GKE create. The check sends the project, the region, and the zones of
    every pool, and fails when Palette refuses them.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: POST
    path: /v1/clouds/gcp/properties/validate
    body: |
      { cloudAccountUid: .entity.spec.cloudAccountUid,
        projectId: .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project,
        region: .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region,
        azs: ([ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]? | (.cloudConfig.azs // [])[] ] | unique) }
    expr: |
      if .response.ok then []
      else ["Palette refuses the project or a zone of the GCP cluster: \(.response.body.code // .response.status): \(.response.body.message // "")"] end
  reproduce:
  - gcloud projects describe {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --format 'value(projectId,lifecycleState)'
  - gcloud compute zones list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter 'region:{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }}' --format 'value(name,status)'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Call the property route of GCP from the cluster validate route.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    note: the property route of GCP reads the project with Cloud Resource Manager and each zone with Compute
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gcp/properties-refused.json
    clean: fixtures/gcp/properties-accepted.json
